.TH CT 1 "October 2026" "ct 4.4.53" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ct - trace Bash command resolution and analyze command conflicts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ct
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIcommand\fR
.br
.B ct
.BR -c
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ct
(Command Trace) analyzes how Bash resolves a command name and, when
an external executable is selected, traces subsequent filesystem and
execution layers.

For a command name,
.B ct
examines Bash command namespaces including aliases, functions, reserved
keywords, builtins, and external executables found through
.BR $PATH .
It can also identify shadowed executables and filesystem indirection
such as symbolic links, alternatives, and usr-merged directory layouts.

.B ct
accounts for Bash command-resolution behavior affected by POSIX mode,
including special-builtin precedence and the enabled or disabled state
of builtins.

For external executable files,
.B ct
can inspect the interpreter specified by an ELF executable or script
shebang and report the relationship between the Bash resolution target
and the eventual execution target.

.B ct
also inspects Bash command hash-table state and can report when a
cached executable path differs from the path resolved through the
current
.BR $PATH .

When normal command resolution fails,
.B ct
can detect and report the presence and source location of Bash's
.B command_not_found_handle
function. The handler is reported but is not invoked by
.B ct .

The command argument must be a bare command name. Pathnames such as
.I /bin/ls
and
.I ./script
are not accepted.

.B ct
operates against the current Bash environment. Its results therefore
depend on aliases, functions, builtins, shell options, POSIX mode, the
Bash command hash table, and
.BR $PATH
visible to the invoking shell.

When
.B -c
is specified,
.B ct
performs environment-wide command conflict analysis instead of tracing
a single command.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Display usage information and exit.

.TP
.BR -v ", " --version
Display version and license information and exit.

.TP
.BR -j ", " --json
Emit structured JSON output suitable for scripting and automation.
JSON output is available for normal command tracing and conflict
analysis.

The JSON schema may change between versions. Fields may be added in
future releases. A JSON
.I null
value indicates that a field is not applicable. Boolean values are
represented as JSON
.I true
or
.I false.

.TP
.BR -x ", " --extend
Force search-path extension during command discovery to include common
system and administrative directories that may not be present in the
current environment.

Normally,
.B ct
automatically extends the search path when a command cannot be found
in the user's
.BR $PATH .
The
.B -x
option forces this extension even when the command has already been
found, allowing
.B ct
to discover executable files that are shadowed by an earlier
.BR $PATH
entry.

The extension is used for discovery and analysis. The original
.BR $PATH
is restored before
.B ct
returns.

.TP
.BR -c ", " --conflict
Run environment-wide command conflict analysis.

This mode identifies command names for which multiple Bash command
definitions are present, including aliases, functions, builtins, and
reserved keywords. External executables are examined separately to
identify executables shadowed by those definitions.

.TP
.BR -cjx
Combined short options may be supplied without separating the flags.
For example,
.B ct -cjx command
is equivalent to supplying
.BR -c ,
.BR -j ,
and
.BR -x
separately.

.SH COMMAND RESOLUTION
When Bash encounters a command name, resolution can involve several
command namespaces.

.B ct
examines:

.IP
aliases
.br
functions
.br
reserved keywords
.br
builtins
.br
external executables
.PP

For external commands,
.B ct
also examines the filesystem and execution layers that follow command
selection.

These may include:

.IP
.BR $PATH
search order
.br
shadowed executables
.br
symbolic-link chains
.br
.BR /etc/alternatives
.br
usr-merged directory relationships
.br
ELF interpreters
.br
script shebangs
.PP

.B ct
also accounts for Bash command-resolution rules affected by POSIX mode,
including the precedence of POSIX special builtins and the enabled or
disabled state of builtins.

This allows
.B ct
to distinguish the command Bash selects from what the operating system
subsequently loads or executes.

.SH RESOLUTION AND EXECUTION TARGETS
For an external command,
.B ct
distinguishes between the target selected by Bash during command
resolution and the executable subsequently loaded or executed.

The
.B Bash Resolution Target
identifies the command selected by Bash, including the selected
filesystem path.

The
.B Kernel Execution Target
identifies the executable reached after filesystem indirection such as
symbolic links or
.BR /etc/alternatives ,
and may include the ELF interpreter or script interpreter involved in
execution.

For shell constructs such as aliases, functions, keywords, and
builtins, no kernel execution target exists.

.SH BASH HASH TABLE
Bash maintains a command hash table containing paths previously found
through
.BR $PATH .

.B ct
inspects this cached state when available and compares a cached
executable path with the path resolved through the current
.BR $PATH .

If the paths differ,
.B ct
reports the discrepancy as a hash-table mismatch.

The hash-table state reported by
.B ct
describes Bash's current cached state. The comparison is performed
against the path
.B ct
resolves through the current search path.

.B ct
does not modify, clear, or rebuild the Bash command hash table.

Changes to
.BR $PATH
may cause Bash to discard remembered command locations. When
.B ct
temporarily changes
.BR $PATH
during path extension, the original
.BR $PATH
and shell state are restored before
.B ct
returns.

.SH COMMAND NOT FOUND HANDLING
If normal Bash command resolution fails,
.B ct
checks whether the current shell defines
.BR command_not_found_handle .

If present,
.B ct
reports that the function is defined and identifies its source location
when available.

.B command_not_found_handle
is a Bash post-resolution mechanism. It is not part of the normal
command-resolution search.

.B ct
reports the handler but does not invoke it during analysis.

The handler itself can be traced as a normal Bash function:

.B
ct command_not_found_handle

.SH PATH DISCOVERY
.B ct
examines
.BR $PATH
in search order and can report executable candidates that are
shadowed by an earlier entry.

An empty component in
.BR $PATH
is treated by Bash as the current working directory.
.B ct
reports such components explicitly during path analysis.

Some system commands reside in administrative directories such as
.I /usr/sbin
or
.I /sbin
that may not be present in an ordinary user's
.BR $PATH .

Normally,
.B ct
automatically extends the search path when a command cannot be found
in the user's
.BR $PATH .

The
.B -x
option forces this path extension even when the command has already
been found, allowing
.B ct
to discover executable files in administrative directories that are
shadowed by an earlier
.BR $PATH
entry.

The original
.BR $PATH
is restored when analysis completes.

.SH CONFLICT MODE
.B ct -c
performs a different analysis from normal command tracing.

It examines command names supplied by the current shell's command
namespaces:

.IP
reserved keywords
.br
aliases
.br
functions
.br
builtins
.PP

It determines which definitions are present for each name and evaluates
their precedence under Bash command-resolution rules, including
relevant POSIX-mode behavior.

External executables are not treated as competing command definitions
in this mode. They are checked only to determine whether an executable
exists that can be shadowed by a shell definition.

For example, an external executable may be reported as
.B external(shadowed)
when a shell-defined command name prevents that executable from being
selected.

Conflict mode is intended to identify command-name collisions within
the current Bash environment. It does not perform the full filesystem
and execution trace performed by normal command tracing.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B ct
operates on the current shell environment and may temporarily modify
shell state while performing analysis.

.B $PATH
may be temporarily extended during discovery.

Temporary modifications are restored before
.B ct
returns.

.B ct
does not permanently modify the invoking shell environment.

.B ct
does not require or enforce
.BR "set -e" ,
.BR "set -u" ,
or
.BR "pipefail"
in the invoking shell.

.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP
Only bare command names are accepted.
.br
Results depend on the current Bash environment.
.br
Filesystem information depends on platform layout and available
permissions.
.br
Bash command hash-table state reflects the invoking shell and may
change when
.BR $PATH
changes.
.br
Conflict analysis may require more time in large or heavily customized
shell environments.
.br
PATH-based executable candidates are not treated as competing command
definitions in conflict mode.
.br
.B ct
does not invoke
.B command_not_found_handle
during analysis.
.br
The kernel execution target describes filesystem and interpreter
indirection visible to
.B ct
and does not trace subsequent processes or system calls performed by
the executed program.

.SH REQUIREMENTS
.B ct
requires:

.IP
Bash 4.4 or newer
.br
GNU/Linux
.br
readlink
.br
readelf
.br
mktemp
.br
stat

.PP
.B tput
is optional and is used for terminal color support when available.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B ct awk
Trace the resolution of
.BR awk .

.TP
.B ct cd
Inspect a shell builtin and other possible command definitions.

.TP
.B ct python
Trace command resolution and inspect executable and filesystem
indirection.

.TP
.B ct -j awk
Produce JSON output for a command resolution trace.

.TP
.B ct -x useradd
Force discovery of administrative executables that may be shadowed by
a command already found in the user's
.BR $PATH .

.TP
.B ct -c
Analyze command-name conflicts in the current Bash environment.

.TP
.B ct -cj
Produce JSON conflict analysis.

.TP
.B ct foobarbaz
Report an unresolved command and, when defined, the current
.B command_not_found_handle
function.

.TP
.B ct command_not_found_handle
Trace the Bash function used for post-resolution command-not-found
handling.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.B ct
returns a non-zero status when command analysis encounters an error or
when an explicitly requested operation cannot be completed.

A non-zero result does not necessarily indicate that the analyzed
command itself is invalid or unusable.

.SH SEE ALSO
